WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.800
Glowing reviews, viral dance routines,&nbsp;
and all the makings of a gay icon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.862
M3GAN managed to defy the odds to turn her&nbsp;
meme-worthy status into a box office hit.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.340
If expectations for "M3GAN" were low, a&nbsp;
big part of that might have been due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.660
its release date. Over the years, January&nbsp;
has become a dumping ground for films that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.980
the studios don't have much faith in. While&nbsp;
bad weather often keeps moviegoers home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.720
financial belt-tightening after the expensive&nbsp;
holiday season is also a factor. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.460
with the last of December's prestige pictures&nbsp;
going into wide release, it's become a truism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.860
that January is the time studios release bombs and&nbsp;
stinkers without anyone paying too much attention.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.320
But as with every rule, there are exceptions. For&nbsp;
instance, in the midst of the January doldrums,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.180
Liam Neeson staked out a claim in the 2010s&nbsp;
with his particular brand of grizzled thrillers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.920
And while the month is often a graveyard of&nbsp;
dreadful comedies and franchise non-starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.800
it has also debuted its share of influential and&nbsp;
innovative horror films, from Robert Rodriguez's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.760
"From Dusk Till Dawn" to the first entries&nbsp;
in the "Hostel" and "Cloverfield" series.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.220
In the first weekend of January 2023,&nbsp;
James Cameron's juggernaut sequel "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.380
The Way of Water" continued to dominate the U.S.&nbsp;
box office for the fourth week in a row. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.180
the number two spot was claimed by "M3GAN,"&nbsp;
the James Wan-produced horror flick about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.140
young orphan whose roboticist aunt gives&nbsp;
her an advanced android made to look and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.340
act like a preteen girl. Cady and her&nbsp;
new robot best friend, nicknamed M3GAN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.140
quickly become inseparable. However, M3GAN&nbsp;
soon takes her programming imperative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.000
to protect Cady from all physical and emotional&nbsp;
dangers, to its violent, logical endpoint.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.740
The film had made a splash months earlier&nbsp;
with a marketing campaign that left audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.480
confused as to what kind of movie "M3gan"&nbsp;
really was. Viewers seemingly weren't sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.340
whether "M3gan" was a horror film, a comedy,&nbsp;
or something in between. The movie's singing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.660
and dancing homicidal robot had all the&nbsp;
makings of a camp icon, but could just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.700
have easily been a disaster in the making. It&nbsp;
turns out, the movie knows exactly what it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.440
which is a slick reinvention of the killer&nbsp;
doll movie that doesn't so much toe the line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.540
between silly and scary as dance and twirl all&nbsp;
over it. Strong reviews, good word of mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.120
and an inescapable online marketing campaign&nbsp;
helped put a $12 million scary movie within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.560
spitting distance of one of the biggest films&nbsp;
of all time. "M3GAN" quickly swiveled from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.520
being a potential bomb to possibly one&nbsp;
of the biggest horror films of the year.

00:02:24.101 --> 00:02:28.740
Befitting the month of its release, "M3GAN" also&nbsp;
sits at the nexus of several horror trends that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.480
don't always have a track record for quality.&nbsp;
On its surface, the film belongs squarely in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:37.560
the "killer doll" genre, home of "Annabelle"&nbsp;
and the "Child's Play" series, where children's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.300
playthings have minds of their own. Of course,&nbsp;
this usually happens via supernatural means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.640
but M3GAN isn't possessed by a malevolent spirit&nbsp;
the way Annabelle and Chucky are. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:53.160
her evil is born of microchips, wires,&nbsp;
and humankind's hubris, which also puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.660
the film in the "killer robot" category. Like&nbsp;
the cowboy robots of the original "Westworld,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.760
M3GAN obeys her programming to a troubling&nbsp;
degree, and like Arnold Schwarzenegger's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.260
reformed cyborg in "Terminator 2: Judgment&nbsp;
Day," she does it all to protect a young child.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.980
But M3GAN is no chubby-faced&nbsp;
toddler like Chucky and Annabelle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.000
nor is she really a doll in the same&nbsp;
sense. She is built like a preteen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.760
maybe just slightly older than her 9-year-old&nbsp;
ward Cady. She walks, she runs, she dances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.220
and she kills, all with the same inscrutable&nbsp;
expression. In that way, the film also plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.960
with the "scary kid" trope, which at its best,&nbsp;
gives us Henry James' "The Turn of the Screw"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.440
and its adaptations like 1961's "The Innocents"&nbsp;
and 2001's "The Others." At its worst, scary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.240
kids are campy to the point of self-sabotage, as&nbsp;
in the endless "Children of the Corn" sequels.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.740
Most of the schlocky films that trade&nbsp;
in the innate creepiness of a child's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.240
blank stare don't have what "M3GAN" has,&nbsp;
and that's a solid creative team guiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.740
the action. Director Gerard Johnstone&nbsp;
cut his comedy-horror teeth on the 2014&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.200
New Zealand indie "Housebound," which he also&nbsp;
wrote. Screenwriter Akela Cooper, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.640
has emerged as one of the hottest names in&nbsp;
horror on the strength of her script for 2021's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.160
"Malignant," another surprise hit that pulls off a&nbsp;
wild premise with style. She has also contributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.320
scripts to "American Horror Story," "Witches of&nbsp;
East End," and the Netflix series "Chambers."

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.720
But arguably the biggest name behind the scenes&nbsp;
of the film is horror maestro James Wan. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.020
the mastermind behind "Dead Silence"&nbsp;
and the "Saw" and "Annabelle" series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.220
he's no stranger to creepy dolls.&nbsp;
Wan, however, is quick to note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.020
this is actually his first killer doll&nbsp;
movie, telling Deadline in January 2023,

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:49.200
"M3GAN" was filmed in New Zealand during the&nbsp;
summer of 2021. Violet McGraw, who plays Cady,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.220
was just ten years old at the time, but&nbsp;
had already racked up an impressive genre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.140
film and TV resume. She appeared as Florence&nbsp;
Pugh's younger self in the Marvel superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.880
blockbuster "Black Widow" and as the younger&nbsp;
Victoria Pedretti in Mike Flanagan's Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.560
series "The Haunting of Hill House."&nbsp;
Stand-up comic Ronny Chieng co-stars as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:13.020
the shortsighted and ultimately doomed CEO of the&nbsp;
toy company that manufactures M3GAN. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.980
"Girls" star Allison Williams picks up&nbsp;
where she left off in "Get Out" and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:22.620
2018 Netflix thriller "The Perfection," building a&nbsp;
reputation for herself as an upscale scream queen.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.980
"I know she's not real, but she is talking&nbsp;
and blinking, and her face is moving."

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:32.640
But the star of any monster movie is, of course,&nbsp;
the monster. With her doll-like face and uncanny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.040
movements that are graceful, robotic, and feral&nbsp;
all at once, fans wondered if M3GAN was real or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:43.440
a CGI creation. On screen, M3GAN is performed&nbsp;
by a preteen dance prodigy from New Zealand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.840
named Amie Donald, outfitted with a prosthetic&nbsp;
mask whose expressions were enhanced by CGI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.640
M3GAN's creepily soothing voice was provided&nbsp;
by former Disney Channel actor Jenna Davis.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.480
"I actually got her voice pretty quickly&nbsp;
and what I wanted her to be like."

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.860
The film is a co-production by Universal&nbsp;
Pictures, Blumhouse Productions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.020
and Wan's Atomic Monster shingle. In the years&nbsp;
since movie theater attendance cratered due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.640
to pandemic restrictions, many studios have&nbsp;
opted to release films directly to streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.260
platforms. Other studios have attempted to&nbsp;
preserve the moviegoing experience and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.400
revenue that goes with it, by releasing&nbsp;
films in both formats at the same time.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.440
"M3GAN," however, is a different story. According&nbsp;
to Deadline, the film was always planned for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.600
big screen. Instead of a simultaneous theatrical&nbsp;
and streaming premiere, Universal gave it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:36.600
good old-fashioned wide release on over 3,500&nbsp;
screens across the country, which is a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:42.120
vote of confidence in 2023. As it turned out, the&nbsp;
film wildly overperformed on its first weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.910
grossing over $35 million in ticket sales when&nbsp;
its estimated performance was nearly half that.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:52.080
By far, the moment that captured the&nbsp;
internet's attention was a short clip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.640
from the film's climax in which M3GAN pauses&nbsp;
in an office hallway to perform an acrobatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.940
mesmerizing dance to the Skatt Brothers' 1979&nbsp;
disco rock hit "Walk the Night." Before long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.800
the dance had taken over TikTok and other&nbsp;
social media sites, with amateur M3GANs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.680
performing their own takes on the killer doll's&nbsp;
moves. By January 9, 2023 the hashtag #M3GAN had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.180
racked up nearly one billion views. At the&nbsp;
film's December 7 premiere in Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:22.980
a dance troupe of eight M3GANs even performed&nbsp;
a red carpet routine to a Taylor Swift song.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:27.240
The dance was not originally part of the&nbsp;
script, however. It was developed on set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:32.160
by director Gerard Johnstone, dancer&nbsp;
Amie Donald, choreographer Jed Brophy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.560
and dance instructor Kylie Norris. In an&nbsp;
interview with toofab.com, Johnstone said

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.800
"You know, I snuck it on the document&nbsp;
to see if anyone would say anything."

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.460
Ultimately, he was relieved that the&nbsp;
knowingly absurd scene was embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.940
for both its spookiness and its silliness, noting,

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.400
"You're definitely walking a fine&nbsp;
line when you do something like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:51.885
and you just hope it's going to pay off."

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:57.660
The wildfire popularity of the M3GAN dance helped&nbsp;
raise the film's public awareness, and other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:02.280
"Avatar," there was little competition in the&nbsp;
first week of January for either eyeballs or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.120
dollars. But it's also true that the film&nbsp;
has received glowing reviews from critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:11.580
and its Rotten Tomatoes score shot straight into&nbsp;
"Certified Fresh" territory with near-unanimous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.500
praise. Early positive notices may well have&nbsp;
brought in skeptical audiences who feared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.000
that the film was nothing more than a 20-second&nbsp;
viral scene with 100 minutes of fluff around it.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.960
The dance scene may not have been in&nbsp;
Cooper's original script, but the tricky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.100
tone that allows it to exist alongside&nbsp;
grisly death scenes is very much present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:34.920
on the page. This is a film that understands&nbsp;
exactly what it is, as RogerEbert.com said,

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.960
While it's more overtly comic than Cooper&nbsp;
and Wan's previous collaboration "Malignant,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.300
both films are fully committed to their premises,&nbsp;
rooting even the goofiest moments in the emotions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:53.160
and anxieties of the character. "M3GAN" is&nbsp;
a movie about a killer doll, but it's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.244
about the way technological advances replace&nbsp;
the human touch, literally or figuratively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.060
and the pressure that society places on women&nbsp;
to be mothers, whether they want to be or not.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.500
"M3GAN" is a center-parted psychopath&nbsp;
dressed like a Victorian schoolgirl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.940
crossed with a 1970s businesswoman. A&nbsp;
four-foot menace whose eyes may be fake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:15.840
but they can still judge everything&nbsp;
she sees. A My Size Barbie come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.620
life with an ice-cold demeanor and a&nbsp;
sassy mouth. Perhaps unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:23.580
before the movie was even released, M3GAN&nbsp;
was declared a new queer horror icon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:28.920
per Out. While Cooper attributed the character's&nbsp;
popularity in the gay community to the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:33.960
themes of found family, others have a more simple&nbsp;
and blunt explanation. As one Twitter user put it,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.480
Before the film was released, author and&nbsp;
academic Joe Vallese spoke to Vox about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:46.380
the character's imagery and her place&nbsp;
in a continuum of "subverted femininity"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.520
that includes recent horror villains&nbsp;
like The Babadook, the deformed mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:56.580
living underground in 2022's "Barbarian," and&nbsp;
Octavia Spencer's "Ma." As Vallese sees it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:00.660
the limitations of sexual expression&nbsp;
in a heteronormative society lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.740
many queer people to identify with the&nbsp;
characters who are considered monsters.

00:10:12.801 --> 00:10:16.683
"They seemed to understand what her&nbsp;
energy was, which was like the best."

00:10:16.683 --> 00:10:19.560
After its first successful&nbsp;
weekend at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:24.420
Universal had already begun talks of a&nbsp;
sequel. Director Gerard Johnstone, for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:28.904
seems excited for the prospect of another&nbsp;
visit to the M3GAN-verse, telling Variety,

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.300
But we may not have to wait for a&nbsp;
brand new film to get more M3GAN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.860
in our lives. According to screenwriter&nbsp;
Akela Cooper, Universal cut a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:48.900
violence and gore out of the film in order&nbsp;
to secure a PG-13 rating, so an expanded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:53.160
R-rated edition may be in the works.&nbsp;
Cooper told the Los Angeles Times,

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:08.880
Hopefully we'll be able to see M3GAN&nbsp;
unleash her full bloody power in the future.

